// `shared`'s parameter `z` is unannotated, so its type is inferred (a Hole).
// It gets called from both `left` (where the in-scope size is `n`) and
// `right` (where it's `m`) as `shared x`/`shared y`, with `x: Idx n` and
// `y: Idx m`. Solving the Hole against `left`'s call site first sets it to
// `Idx n`; checking it again against `right`'s call site then has to compare
// `n` (free w.r.t. this second, independent check -- it's `left`'s bound
// variable, out of scope inside `right`) against `m` (bound in `right`).
// These are two DIFFERENT variables, so the check should fail -- but it
// doesn't. mim then goes on to construct `foo`'s body referencing `n` (a
// variable local to `left`) even though `foo` itself is `extern` and is
// therefore required to be a CLOSED term. That invariant violation is only
// ever noticed later, in World::Externals::externalize, as a raw
// `assert(def->is_closed())` -- i.e. a crash with no source location, not a
// diagnosed compile error.
//
// Root cause: Checker::alpha_'s free-variable case (src/mim/check.cpp,
// around line 246):
//
//     // both var1 and var2 are free: OK, when they are the same or in Check mode
//     return var1 == var2 || mode == Check;
//
// In `Check` mode (used while solving Holes during type inference), any two
// Vars that are "free" from the perspective of the current comparison are
// treated as compatible -- even when they are literally different variables
// bound by different, unrelated binders (here: `left`'s `n` vs `right`'s
// `m`). There is no occurs/scope check verifying that a Hole's solution only
// mentions variables that are actually in scope where the Hole originated.
// Confirmed live by instrumenting that line: the comparison that gets waved
// through right before the crash is exactly
//   var1 = <right's own bound var>, var2 = <left's own bound var>.
//
// This is not specific to this program shape -- the same permissive branch
// fires constantly during ordinary elaboration (e.g. comparing an axiom's
// own generic parameter name against an unrelated one at a use site), so it
// is presumably relied upon elsewhere; a real fix likely needs an actual
// scope/level check on Hole resolution rather than just tightening this
// branch outright.
//
// NOT FIXED YET: this should be a clean type/scope error ("cannot unify n
// and m"); it currently crashes instead. Pinned as a crash so the suite
// notices if the failure mode ever changes -- promote to a plain `RUN:
// not %mim ... | FileCheck` (clean error) once fixed.

// RUN: not --crash %mim %s -o - 2>&1 | FileCheck %s

plugin core;

fun extern foo (b: Bool) = (left, right)#b (3, 0_3) where
  con left (n: Nat, x: Idx n) = shared x;
  con right (m: Nat, y: Idx m) = shared y;
  con shared (z) = return 0;
end;

// CHECK: Assertion
